ght, we proceed as follows:\n\n- Extend the line segment $\\overset{―}{PR}.$\n\n![](images/66/664c9507be2e02908b8a151d0c58222545a84a7d71648c28e61badfb23e1981b.png)\n\n- Draw the perpendicular from $Q$ to $\\overset{⟷}{PR}.$\n\n![](images/89/89bc576f142925d0ef639e3a2ade1da2900e24f920e2469fe159b6a8eda238c7.png)"} :step/id #uuid "c929565b-1961-5ca1-a131-800f5679fb16" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567134}}] :activity/title "Heights of Triangles" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566354}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180403} :activity/id #uuid "5af1262a-1c56-58b3-9acb-3b64b5f1eea9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049066 :topic/id #uuid "d72fac68-834e-516a-9e98-9df9b0527be6" :topic/math-academy-id 3748} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180401 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179680 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179539 :question/id #uuid "20f6391d-7ada-51b2-a384-f12a31a622c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-8053"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1e9abbc-b0b5-51a6-b23b-a32a027382f3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563665}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations Containing an Expression Raised to an Odd Power"} :step/id #uuid "52ee7c85-875f-5a5f-af14-c666ff0b02e9" :step/math-academy-id 30069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180402 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179681 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179540 :question/id #uuid "26f0aea2-1440-5e96-9013-8238214b4966" :question/math-academy-id "e-8065"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98f98657-c9c6-5b38-825c-992bcf28d9e3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048187}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563756}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations With Odd Exponents"} :step/id #uuid "247ebd22-9eec-58a6-b24f-de774c47432e" :step/math-academy-id 30121 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180401}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180403 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180684 :tutorial/content "How do we solve an equation with a variable raised to an odd exponent, such as\n\n$$\nx^3 = 125?\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation by taking the $3$rd root of each side and solving for $x.$ \n\nSince $n=3$ is odd, there will be only one real solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3} & =125 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{x}^{3}} & =\\sqrt[3]{125} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{{5}^{3}} \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=5.$\n\nIn general, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n*When $n$ is odd, and we take the $n$th root of both sides of $x^n = a,$ we get only one solution:* \n\n$$\nx = \\sqrt[n]{a}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a3deb3f5-8f54-5126-a222-4b9346b3c89f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4834 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c7f88cee-5451-591c-bcf6-e4c817bdbf06" :step/math-academy-id 30129 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180402}}] :activity/title "Solving Equations With Odd Exponents Using the Nth Root Method" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179891}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563811} :activity/id #uuid "ab25c004-2d05-58f2-a0d6-54009af95a70" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049066 :topic/id #uuid "d72fac68-834e-516a-9e98-9df9b0527be6" :topic/math-academy-id 3748} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563767 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563665 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25486f62-97da-5109-9737-87f525d30db1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563029} {:answer/id #uuid "2c8f8e6f-e697-5bb7-bf75-2e4331631d5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563046} {:answer/id #uuid "51605067-0787-53ee-aaf2-ba1451c7298b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563130} {:answer/id #uuid "6fcb20f0-7b84-5ab6-9881-9ca58e2d8984" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563198} {:answer/id #uuid "a22620b9-3627-519a-9fcf-882b66fb2509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563311}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563311} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dda18e4-efa2-53cb-b386-72e8b9f7b0ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "351787b8-999c-59c8-b44f-8fe6c60247fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-91369" :question/problem "Find the real solution to the equation $(x-3)^{7}=-128.$" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this equation by taking the $7$th root of each side and solving for $x.$\nSince $n=7$ is odd, there is only one real solution to this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{7} & =-128 \\\\ \\sqrt[7]{(x-3)^{7}} & =\\sqrt[7]{-128} \\\\ x-3 & =\\sqrt[7]{(-2)^{7}} \\\\ x-3 & =-2 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=1.$"} :step/id #uuid "1bd75efd-e9f9-5e75-ad2e-b07553799349"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563804 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563756 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "097ae540-a1c3-563f-958f-879f687d07c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562959} {:answer/id #uuid "27ed339f-1ba9-5371-b98d-4f3e5a4957d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563033} {:answer/id #uuid "4b16ec7e-f0ec-57c4-b0d0-1fe68110981c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563110} {:answer/id #uuid "7356e8b2-c698-5246-b83e-adcafee91941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563201} {:answer/id #uuid "7375c3cd-5447-5289-b4b3-d26964bf0961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "There is no real solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563202}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562959} :answer-field/id #uuid "76b0b8d7-ead0-5e0b-96f7-c011cf47b3ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ee997e26-088f-52b1-8913-d296b8992e92" :question/math-academy-id "q-91348" :question/problem "Find the real solution to the equation ${x}^{7}=-\\frac{1}{128}.$" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this equation by taking the $7$th root of each side and solving for $x.$ Since $n=7$ is odd, there is only one real solution to this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{7} & =-\\frac{1}{128} \\\\ \\sqrt[7]{{x}^{7}} & =\\sqrt[7]{(-\\frac{1}{128})} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[7]{{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{7}} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "d915e072-457e-5546-ab5a-73371243b5c8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563767}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563811 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180296 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2f71584-e930-53ee-b586-0e3133771dab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179321} {:answer/id #uuid "5ab8c055-ea9d-5540-81ab-2dd3afb58dc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179322} {:answer/id #uuid "5e8b8815-feff-5dbb-b722-410093a45a1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179323} {:answer/id #uuid "699e6966-53d4-5218-bca9-f122c0e0dc40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179324} {:answer/id #uuid "7253e80a-a034-5926-8005-bbcfd67e0155" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179325}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179321} :answer-field/id #uuid "f47acc13-93e7-5be5-891c-f394fc5d2e8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c66a4d95-de66-5d58-9163-57438e7f73ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-90945" :question/problem "Find the real solution to the equation $2(x+4)^{3}=686.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We can solve this equation by isolating the $(x+4)^{3}$ term and then taking the $3$rd root of each side.\n\nFirst, let's isolate the $(x+4)^{3}$ term on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+4)^{3} & =686 \\\\ (x+4)^{3} & =343\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the $3$rd root of each side and solve for $x.$ Since $n=3$ is odd, there is only one real solution to this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{3} & =343 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(x+4)^{3}} & =\\sqrt[3]{343} \\\\ x+4 & =\\sqrt[3]{{7}^{3}} \\\\ x+4 & =7 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=3.$"} :step/id #uuid "e8f889b2-3513-5a19-aa94-03ceb44dba6e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563804}}] :activity/title "Solving Equations With Odd Exponents Using the Nth Root Method" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562934}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176598} :activity/id #uuid "ec9d4c2b-1a22-5786-bdab-76e3e5ac092b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049135 :topic/id #uuid "dd916270-3c49-5d1a-a09d-9c318a1004ba" :topic/math-academy-id 3565} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176598 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176967 :tutorial/content "Let's consider yet more important parts of a circle:\n\n- The **circumference** of a circle is used to describe the perimeter of the circle, as shown in the left diagram below.\n\n- An **arc** is a part of the circumference which is enclosed between two points on a circle. In the right diagram below, we have the arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}.$\n\n![](images/5c/5c918b32007a68beecfdef486e9cd5d0818a2122ad672415608f761f7f7953e7.png)\n\nIf an arc is denoted using only two letters, we are referring to the *shortest* arc. However, to avoid ambiguity, we can use three letters. For example, in the diagram below, $\\overset{\\frown}{ACB}$ is the arc to the left and $\\overset{\\frown}{ADB}$ is the arc to the right.\n\n![](images/f6/f6329cc70c467e968628d2d82a0839253ee443881371893d6d7721724e9ae02a.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "8e47b911-fb88-5251-8c9f-e05a36b02014" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2419 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "25d2b401-6584-5b07-9e4c-0c4734e5d10a" :step/math-academy-id 10841 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176670}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176599 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175896 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175763 :question/id #uuid "2c4c8545-1b0a-519d-95b5-55c1da11b443" :question/math-academy-id "e-3169"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1941876-94d1-52d5-9104-7875de691ca0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049856}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504675}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of a Circle"} :step/id #uuid "9822102f-f61a-5ba7-a66f-4d48d24c1c3e" :step/math-academy-id 10842 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176600}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176600 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175897 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175764 :question/id #uuid "11cc1b43-3faf-5687-aca8-12c3e60befca" :question/math-academy-id "e-3170"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "938c4422-a8ae-56c4-b728-31776db43653" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176523} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504670}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing a True Statement Regarding Circles"} :step/id #uuid "86752c7b-de72-5f68-969c-03022173a2d1" :step/math-academy-id 10843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176670 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176974 :tutorial/content "Finally, we have two more elements of a circle to remember:\n\n- A **circular sector** is the region bounded by an arc of the circle and two radii which connect the center of the circle with the endpoints of the arc. In the left diagram below, we have the circular sector $OCD.$\n\n- A **circular segment** is the region bounded by an arc of the circle and the chord that connects the endpoints of the arc. In the right diagram below, we have the circular segment $PQ.$\n\n![](images/84/848e64be2b80b78d88da42473f6778d4012f7ec52ae6950f4ca3635b5b154ca6.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "3f8e3ddf-673b-5e4a-9d61-f8a2c682ee36" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3986 :tutorial/title "Circular Segments and Sectors"} :step/id #uuid "6b12863c-3e31-50e4-b812-6ce26cac6267" :step/math-academy-id 23718 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176599}}] :activity/title "Arcs, Segments, and Sectors of Circles" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176115}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129928} :activity/id #uuid "4c188df7-e524-54c2-8a5d-b2f16e8dfb77" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049472 :topic/id #uuid "f7e72507-2c32-5978-b2b5-175e533dfdf0" :topic/math-academy-id 2022} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129928 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130482 :tutorial/content "The **roots** of a function are the values of the input $x$ for which the function equals zero. For this reason, roots are sometimes also called **zeros**.\n\nAlgebraically, the roots of a function are the solutions for $x$ in the following equation:\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\nFor example, if $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2}x+1,$ then we can find the roots by solving the equation $f(x) = 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x+1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =-1 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, this function has a single root: $x=-2.$\n\nGraphically, the roots of a function are the $x$-intercepts. For example, for the graph of $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2}x+1$ shown below, we can see that the function intercepts the $x$-axis at $x=-2.$\n\n![](images/a7/a75e8a0ae40c5cc940f9fe2d9194b0dcb5c6db2742e87a6d903dc15c0b970e99.png)\n\nWhen shown on a graph, the roots of a function are also called the $\\boldsymbol x$**-intercepts.**" :tutorial/id #uuid "37a5be36-f2b1-5ebb-91b6-98ccbc9babaa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8802 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "14413a03-0cc3-58a8-9a8d-a19f98f83fa5" :step/math-academy-id 146046 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129929}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129929 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129106 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128873 :question/id #uuid "c1e36290-8271-5508-9bf8-cf15a3907f75" :question/math-academy-id "e-14174"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82f5682f-73b8-5c0a-b7d3-3336200843a0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048838}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503696}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Function Given Its Graph"} :step/id #uuid "b4ec4b62-fcff-5a84-8aff-d4e6e77464a0" :step/math-academy-id 146047 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129930}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129930 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129107 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128874 :question/id #uuid "a443151c-f310-533e-adb7-1c8eda168738" :question/math-academy-id "e-14176"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02e29602-b3c0-5ceb-ab65-710c06a08c1d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557329}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Function Given a Table"} :step/id #uuid "5b6a8ba5-7be7-53d6-ab4c-470a3ff5b6a6" :step/math-academy-id 146050 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129931}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129931 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129108 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128875 :question/id #uuid "c92eb920-7734-57fd-9f34-c1917d435eaf" :question/math-academy-id "e-14177"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "578132af-f322-5a68-9771-72e1954622ad" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564482}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Function Given a Mapping Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "9541d048-63ed-5e4b-b3a2-c1f4afb2e23c" :step/math-academy-id 146053 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129932}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129932 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129109 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128876 :question/id #uuid "d5e99647-7ada-562f-8a16-a61f4d1ae0be" :question/math-academy-id "e-14179"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82d522a8-ae3c-5723-909e-540ddac1d9da" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048673}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129816} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503698} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522763}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots Given a Function Definition"} :step/id #uuid "459f52ce-a337-5d29-918d-c439315a2395" :step/math-academy-id 146056}] :activity/title "The Roots of a Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129364}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557405} :activity/id #uuid "ad1504c9-2cb0-5fbd-8072-a67bb0a595b0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049472 :topic/id #uuid "f7e72507-2c32-5978-b2b5-175e533dfdf0" :topic/math-academy-id 2022} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557361 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "989608c8-9218-5c75-88f5-8e4e4b9c8cf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$40$ and $70$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556796} {:answer/id #uuid "9b5ae992-2c54-5206-802e-b1b2ca2238ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$10$ and $80$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556809} {:answer/id #uuid "aa81c40a-29e9-5b90-aa31-f1b514f34e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$50$ and $60$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556851} {:answer/id #uuid "c2dbdc2f-39d6-538b-9826-8f355d259ab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$20$ and $50$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556901} {:answer/id #uuid "d463ad01-7754-59b7-9f2e-30cf3c021ff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$30$ and $60$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556946}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556796} :answer-field/id #uuid "112eb8e3-a60e-524c-8724-a81064b58bcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "30380421-4837-53f2-affd-65b4ad08f0c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-82668" :question/problem "If $g(x)$ is given by the table shown below, what are its roots?\n\n| $x$ | $10$ | $20$ | $30$ | $40$ | $50$ | $60$ | $70$ | $80$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $5$ | $15$ | $25$ | $0$ | $-15$ | $-25$ | $0$ | $5$ |" :question/worked-solution "The roots of the function are the values of $x$ such that $g(x)=0.$ In the given table, we see that $g(40)=0$ and $g(70)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the roots of the function are $x=40$ and $x=70.$"} :step/id #uuid "5de8eb33-d802-54e5-aa96-cdcc2203cde7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557375}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557375 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557329 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ed1228a-7373-5a96-877e-e5c7fe47067d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $5$ | $10$ | $12$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556605} {:answer/id #uuid "505f7fd0-1497-5427-8ea5-504d5959d49b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556611} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f69b99-1c6d-5613-9d2e-27ffb7439256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-4$ | $-5$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556830} {:answer/id #uuid "a88c84c6-3f5e-5722-b013-555a318561ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-3$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556848} {:answer/id #uuid "fbcb6e52-0b1e-5682-8a11-a9bdab6b8164" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $4$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-5$ | $-7$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557053}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557053} :answer-field/id #uuid "03f14d9d-05ea-5eba-9f15-106f65b248f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f7198a9b-72ee-54fc-b7ac-cda83b608de8" :question/math-academy-id "q-82670" :question/problem "Which of the following tables describes a function that has $0$ as a root?" :question/worked-solution "The number $0$ is a root of $f(x)$ if $f(0)=0.$ Of the given tables, only the following table has this property:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $4$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-5$ | $-7$ |"} :step/id #uuid "86ef8295-2490-501a-9c7c-8f68fdb3f21a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557405 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557303 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cd9eaf3-ed30-5541-af86-8a86b83910ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$0$ and $\\frac{1}{7}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556496} {:answer/id #uuid "2e59e379-49a8-54d1-a572-f41f95712c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)$ has no roots" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556529} {:answer/id #uuid "6e2ccad8-734d-582e-b7c7-0ed5544f1b87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{7}$ and $\\frac{1}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556683} {:answer/id #uuid "8790a791-e8bb-5896-87ed-e82e9ab0674d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556749} {:answer/id #uuid "956e4771-73f0-5b6f-980d-af1813caa099" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-4$ and $-\\frac{1}{9}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556789}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556749} :answer-field/id #uuid "e84544a2-76c0-5219-9d3a-4068e68e816b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c306bf08-706c-5a8c-b907-b249d9ec68eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-37888" :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef42744182deb0d83704b13fba2324b92df197151c3566de01bb75a202e836cc.png)\n\nWhat are the roots of the function $f(x)$ defined by the mapping diagram shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The roots of the function are the values of $x$ such that $f(x)=0.$ In the given mapping diagram, we see that $f(\\frac{1}{3})=0.$\n\nTherefore, the only root of the function is $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$"} :step/id #uuid "efc74aa4-b0b2-5917-86c7-5993bf2370b5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557361}}] :activity/title "The Roots of a Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556417}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1440.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502186} :activity/id #uuid "33615341-898b-5ad2-95e1-3bdfee51b554" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050012 :topic/id #uuid "2616710e-7f3d-5028-b0fe-1ed436c185da" :topic/math-academy-id 1024} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502176 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502066 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502129 :question/id #uuid "9c400da4-eba5-58aa-8ee5-a33269adfa2a" :question/math-academy-id "e-22728"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b6c3c0b-b541-5b1f-810b-d9c4ffa1fe9c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502155}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems Involving Dividing Negative Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "3a846be7-1beb-5415-afda-c7dbc9dfc761" :step/math-academy-id 621937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502186 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502216 :tutorial/content "Some real-world situations involve a quantity changing by the same amount repeatedly.\n\nWhen the same change occurs repeatedly, we can represent the total change using multiplication.\n\nFor example, suppose a submarine begins at sea level and descends $2\\,\\text{m}$ each minute for $4$ minutes.\n\nSince descending represents a decrease in elevation, the change each minute is $-2\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nThe total change can be written as repeated subtraction:\n\n![](images/bd/bd6e5218d475c46dd7f0ac578340124954d00fa940c81e32bffa49638b0e79c3.svg)\n\nThis repeated subtraction can also be written using multiplication:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}4} \\times (-2)\n$$\n\nEvaluating the product, we get\n\n$$\n4 \\times (-2) = -8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $4$ minutes, the submarine's elevation is $-8\\,\\text{m}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e61ac714-2900-508a-9e66-9731f70e9997" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15164 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "71ab7d7f-9cc1-5cdf-9b66-2a332d38a8be" :step/math-academy-id 621932 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502187}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502187 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502067 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502160 :question/id #uuid "f100ae8f-6865-5741-a125-4d55e90e2e43" :question/math-academy-id "e-22713"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d7b67c1-aa22-5106-9f09-19e361dc25c0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502136}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems Involving Multiplying Negative Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "75f9f2c1-6398-5c11-a04e-b2c1ba8f749f" :step/math-academy-id 621933 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502188}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502188 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502068 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502105 :question/id #uuid "5f3cfae7-b7d6-56b2-a245-6ec44b76e462" :question/math-academy-id "e-22714"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aec0cd94-20e4-5748-b9c1-4de7b1854a8e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502156}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems Involving Multiplying Negative Fractions"} :step/id #uuid "7899018c-d6d5-5a73-8bba-a499b1170d83" :step/math-academy-id 621941 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502210}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502209 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502215 :tutorial/content "Sometimes we know the total change in a quantity and want to find how much the quantity changes during each equal time period.\n\nIn these situations, we use division.\n\nFor example, suppose a total of $\\[math]24$ is withdrawn from a bank account over $6$ days in equal amounts.\n\nSince money is withdrawn, the total change in the balance is $-\\[/math]24$.\n\nTo find the change each day, we divide the total change by the number of days:\n\n$$\n-24 \\div 6\n$$\n\nEvaluating the quotient, we get\n\n$$\n-24 \\div 6 = -4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the balance changes by $-\\[math]4$ each day." :tutorial/id #uuid "2472ba13-0b69-56f2-a477-ea1b2a6fc7e3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15182 :tutorial/title "Solving Problems Using Division of Rational Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "f88bb973-cdd9-5dc3-a6f0-ee3f3807638e" :step/math-academy-id 621978 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502176}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502210 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502069 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502120 :question/id #uuid "7be163d0-2103-5467-9bba-a4090855cc8b" :question/math-academy-id "e-22729"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f589fffd-f5c4-590e-9ed1-01c20390700f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502119}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems Involving Multiplying Negative Fractions and Mixed Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "f8abbf23-8275-57f4-b19a-767b1595b9d0" :step/math-academy-id 621944 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502209}}] :activity/title "Solving Problems Using Multiplication and Division of Rational Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092407}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069739} :activity/id #uuid "4207750e-9221-5674-acec-1d120f06d3d3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050229 :topic/id #uuid "37df2ecc-fe37-586e-8075-7eb42347c2ba" :topic/math-academy-id 458} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069707 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068787 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068631 :question/id #uuid "489ef834-7015-5592-a5f5-b0daa3bc9f7b" :question/math-academy-id "e-6278"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6619614-04df-5a22-afa8-77a41b016330" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505808}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Union of Overlapping Intervals Given Graphically"} :step/id #uuid "d06f5800-1be7-5609-b455-6c1d38707d7d" :step/math-academy-id 23592 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069883}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069739 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069934 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to represent the following number line graph using interval notation:\n\n![](images/0b/0bf5e420e2929032c11964e15d710014fb34d559aa065428b9d57fff14d10eb0.png)\n\nThis number line consists of the interval $(-3, -1)$ as well as the interval $(1, 3).$\n\nTo express the combination of these two intervals, we create a **union of intervals** using the **union sign** $\\cup$ as follows: \n\n$$\n(-3, -1) \\cup (1, 3)\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3a3780a2-b10d-5640-a797-ea56e4eee1af" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1422 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b0fb12dd-e8a8-5a10-9f60-f1d994766e86" :step/math-academy-id 3167 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069740}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069740 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068811 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068666 :question/id #uuid "d0e51ce4-7f83-545e-9c3c-b79bf58d0eab" :question/math-academy-id "e-952"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eee95dd4-7d0c-5c18-bcb0-9b033e0cb509" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049162}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505781}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing an Inequality as a Union of Intervals"} :step/id #uuid "325b8527-c92b-5ecd-b56e-5c14cc0b6cf5" :step/math-academy-id 3168 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069741}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069741 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068812 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068667 :question/id #uuid "c575830c-0100-5cec-a3f8-70e29cba3b54" :question/math-academy-id "e-954"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1300ab0b-313f-5489-a836-79cf200082f6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505816}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Union of Non-Overlapping Intervals as an Inequality"} :step/id #uuid "655fb4ff-9084-5ff0-9f76-e5c230ef695e" :step/math-academy-id 3172 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069884}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069883 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068906 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068521 :question/id #uuid "9cf4daed-1d80-5a74-90dc-b0b1da0ce6b9" :question/math-academy-id "e-1695"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a74d2eb-fe22-5c2d-b31f-9446f8f080d3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505839}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Union of Overlapping Intervals"} :step/id #uuid "5832944b-422f-5154-8451-6dc2929473ea" :step/math-academy-id 5562} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069884 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069950 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, the union of two intervals can simplify into a single interval. For example, consider the union\n\n$$\n(-3,2) \\cup (0,4).\n$$\n\nIf we plot these two intervals on number lines, we get the following:\n\n![](images/4a/4aafe0cdf9f5b27d99538bbaacbf567ded823189399e518f2ead7381da35e84c.png)\n\n![](images/4a/4a26c3aa30f2aab862b31624ba08e3a575fee511e46f89abf62c26aa38dd811f.png)\n\nThe union of the two intervals consists of all the points which are contained within at least one of the intervals. So, to graph the union of the two intervals, we take the graphs of the individual intervals and lay them on top of each other.\n\nThe resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/83/835f7e883cf1aba1945f9cecab382ddc163e1c2413895c8b2f8d1d61ae81d1a0.png)\n\nThis graph represents the interval $(-3,4).$ So, the union $(-3,2) \\cup (0,4)$ simplifies to the interval $(-3,4).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a4af4ad6-6c9c-552b-8ab9-8cb92744e7c6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1798 :tutorial/title "The Union of Overlapping Intervals"} :step/id #uuid "423acfad-41bd-5f38-909c-92b01da8da23" :step/math-academy-id 5564 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069707}}] :activity/title "Unions of Intervals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068985}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088504} :activity/id #uuid "16a4de81-848b-5638-b998-ac9062204da4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050245 :topic/id #uuid "3913d667-ede5-5fee-ba67-ff64afeb7883" :topic/math-academy-id 837} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088504 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088989 :tutorial/content "Two distinct lines that never intersect are said to be **parallel.** For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope. \n\nFor example, the lines $y=2x+4$ and $y=2x-5$ have the same slope of $m=2,$ so they are parallel. Indeed, looking at the graph, we see that the two lines never cross.\n\n![](images/c7/c7d36372d9646d0ce10eff5d35a4f9b8e56a7b37b333c5d53ac243065ee751af.png)\n\nOn the other hand, the lines $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+4$ and $y=2x-5$ have different slopes, so they are *not* parallel. Indeed, looking at the graph, we see that the two lines *do* cross.\n\n![](images/92/92c1ee39ccbbbf20dc3f4a7f76a1114d975fb0826eeac13d273b4b4fd9a83105.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "2a7600a0-ce9a-5126-866d-bc3d1cf82b41" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8902 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cab8d9d2-3173-5553-86f8-e9fb1aecfd16" :step/math-academy-id 144181 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088505}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088505 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087686 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087433 :question/id #uuid "4f0593d8-bce5-5c73-aedd-ffe0faf15a59" :question/math-academy-id "e-14364"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "353a2fb8-5677-5047-8f39-595f43d9eeb9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049716}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477090} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502163}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parallel Lines Given in Slope-Intercept Form"} :step/id #uuid "212176f8-846a-5cf2-ae65-698c2a23cb29" :step/math-academy-id 144182 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088506}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088506 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087687 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087434 :question/id #uuid "b2f31785-e173-518c-a1fd-b069e0834b2a" :question/math-academy-id "e-14367"} :knowledge-point/id #