/ref 17592186087910}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106553} :activity/id #uuid "a6ad28b0-f9e7-5bdd-80d5-adf3fcee2856" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050514 :topic/id #uuid "4fadec48-6c17-5c63-a22a-02d845645b15" :topic/math-academy-id 1322} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106553 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107161 :tutorial/content "A **regular polygon** is a polygon where all sides are congruent (have the same length), and all interior angles are congruent (have the same measure).\n\nSome examples of regular polygons are shown below.\n\n![](images/d3/d33f23d3d3e301e336d7f70f37e88eee0f605c31b098969805d7c6346e34470e.png)\n\nSome examples of polygons that are **NOT** regular are as follows:\n\n- The rectangle below is **NOT** regular since the sides are not all congruent.\n\n![](images/6d/6d0c6eaee1ae1932d3bd44013c0b8aca977cbe0d98242d5c10aaa24d4f281a77.png)\n\n- The rhombus below is **NOT** regular since the interior angles are not all congruent.\n\n![](images/ce/ce2b908945db56934f08dc0da47f6c708f5220bce3a80f5147362c8b915c5613.png)\n\n- A $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle is **NOT** regular since neither the sides nor the angles are all congruent.\n\n![](images/bf/bfe00eb1d1374252fd12f6c74eff7c052021a7305ce10027efc8869f561e3ef5.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "cd59495c-0fc7-5db8-8d91-05d02fa8e26f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7926 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f6dcb244-ba60-524d-bafc-0fd7ed3097aa" :step/math-academy-id 119188 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106554}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106554 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105696 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105470 :question/id #uuid "fbeb62a5-1a2c-53df-ab8d-6aaee87c4a11" :question/math-academy-id "e-12647"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9f78a76-4d2f-5694-971c-e734bc330602" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050164}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507524}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regular Polygons"} :step/id #uuid "91bb4d37-5646-5ecb-b152-184d3a1e7df5" :step/math-academy-id 119189 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106555}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106555 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107160 :tutorial/content "![](images/24/24c4c952103f245cd4dc32e34d5dd2363478f5400ddecfbe2503130135c01946.png)\n\nConsider the regular pentagon above. What is the measure of each interior angle?\n\nRecall that the sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals $180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2).$ \n\nAll interior angles in a regular polygon are congruent. So, to calculate the measure of each interior angle, we find the sum of the interior angles and divide by the number of angles.\n\nA pentagon has $n=5$ sides. Therefore, the sum of its interior angles is\n\n$$\n180^\\circ \\times (5 - 2) = 540^\\circ.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the measure of each interior angle is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{540^\\circ}{5} = 108^\\circ,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/a7/a74a23a8dd760a31a0888eb694e36978c4767fd32bca6fae4fe8fe380c85e2e1.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "d7abf1fc-4082-5a4f-a8ea-a74559737f2d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7912 :tutorial/title "Interior Angles of Regular Polygons"} :step/id #uuid "542a4e61-51eb-5729-8e5c-2bd1bfdc8f6b" :step/math-academy-id 119192 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106556}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106556 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105697 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105467 :question/id #uuid "f617d0de-cdcb-5d15-aa91-1d3e6f5edb60" :question/math-academy-id "e-12627"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e475613-ff86-5655-a966-121d622b0bd7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049223}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508044}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Interior Angles of a Regular Polygon"} :step/id #uuid "b1672354-ac07-5ce6-aea9-42ade75a5756" :step/math-academy-id 119193 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106559}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106557 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105698 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105468 :question/id #uuid "2fff10bc-b4a2-5f6c-b1a2-2fa2c5510f60" :question/math-academy-id "e-12628"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5231e20f-0e57-517c-bae9-3a152234d5ab" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050650}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507469} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508109}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Exterior Angles of a Regular Polygon"} :step/id #uuid "5963d13b-c8a9-5e42-9832-539b63c97ffd" :step/math-academy-id 119196 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106558}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106558 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105699 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105469 :question/id #uuid "97bdacef-ecd4-52da-9fef-b8e9233f21a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-12629"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "372bcfad-79d0-5758-a66b-efeacfb13762" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048174}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507500}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exterior Angles With Multiple Polygons"} :step/id #uuid "6be9e91b-24ac-5965-9577-4a57a3357b17" :step/math-academy-id 119199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106559 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107165 :tutorial/content "![](images/8e/8e128a2320d2658cc61cf83a1871d09caa7e4527b684c2af9086f80a00ecc717.png)\n\nConsider the regular hexagon above. What is the measure of each *exterior* angle?\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The exterior angles in a regular polygon are all congruent.\n\n- The sum of the exterior angles of *any* convex polygon equals $360^\\circ.$\n\nSo, to find the measure of the exterior angles of a regular polygon, we divide $360^\\circ$ by the number of sides.\n\nSince a regular hexagon has $n=6$ sides, the measure of each exterior angle is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{360^\\circ}{6} = 60^\\circ,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/4f/4f4538d9e2e09715fa3aa2035cd8b22cdc326b0ac02326551d193f26f1946473.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "754fe990-2a6b-5799-bb6d-bd83d8b8fff4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9042 :tutorial/title "Exterior Angles of Regular Polygons"} :step/id #uuid "60bdea8a-315f-547f-a828-49aa84b406ef" :step/math-academy-id 119202 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106557}}] :activity/title "Regular Polygons" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105976}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205035} :activity/id #uuid "de17f2e1-7eda-5a05-b05f-fc9631097dd2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050884 :topic/id #uuid "6e36fc4f-7ad7-519b-88fa-4bc240d36c28" :topic/math-academy-id 5572} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205035 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205174 :tutorial/content "Some linear equations can have **no solutions.** This means there is no value of $x$ that can be substituted into the equation to give a true statement.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following equation:\n\n$$\nx+1 = x\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to solve this equation. We start by subtracting $x$ from both sides.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =x \\\\ x+1\\,-\\,x & =x\\,-\\,x \\\\ (x-x)+1 & =(x-x) \\\\ 0+1 & =0 \\\\ 1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the final equation, $1=0,$ is always **false**. In other words, there is no value of $x$ that we can substitute to make the equation true. \n\nTherefore, our equation has no solutions." :tutorial/id #uuid "ae552721-c050-5d83-9cfc-347accbc894b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 11390 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1b8a4ffe-a6bc-5748-9925-775d621ef0ae" :step/math-academy-id 476517 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205036}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205036 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204089 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203852 :question/id #uuid "fd07ad0f-548a-5569-84de-d658a88c4b9c" :question/math-academy-id "e-17087"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6226fdb-ee73-5a78-87d5-9d6a988a9eb4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048213}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476177} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204600} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508100}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation With No Solutions"} :step/id #uuid "2a517a1e-2402-5834-92c6-d387d20f3740" :step/math-academy-id 476518 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205037}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205037 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205208 :tutorial/content "In general, suppose we have an equation in the variable $x$ of the form \n\n$$\nax = b,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $a$ and $b$ are constants, and\n\n- $b$ is *not* equal to zero, which we write as $b \\neq 0.$\n\nThen, if $a = 0,$ the equation has **no solutions.**\n\nIndeed, by substituting $a = 0$ into $ax=b,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax & =b \\\\ (0)x & =b \\\\ 0 & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a *contradiction* (i.e., a statement that's always false) because we were told that $b \\neq 0.$ Therefore, the equation has no solution." :tutorial/id #uuid "8a5cc3be-1b56-5e46-8944-f75697b65b59" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13428 :tutorial/title "Conditions for an Equation To Have No Solutions"} :step/id #uuid "a4d784bb-4dc8-51b7-9c57-1caf81eeaf95" :step/math-academy-id 476521 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205038}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205038 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205207 :tutorial/content "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n7qx = -5,\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solutions, what is the value of $q?$\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = b{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{7q}_{\\large a} \\ x = \\underbrace{-5}_{\\large b}\n$$\n\nIn this case,\n\n$$\na = 7q, \\qquad b = -5.\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form $ax=b$ with $b \\neq 0$ has no solutions when $a = 0.$\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n7q = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7q & =0 \\\\ \\frac{7q}{7} & =\\frac{0}{7} \\\\ q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation $7qx = -5$ has no solutions if $q = 0.$\n\n**Note:** The constant $q$ in this problem is sometimes called a **parameter.**" :tutorial/id #uuid "d31382b3-ae61-5a9e-a3bf-53f71f939a5d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13402 :tutorial/title "Problems Involving Unknown Parameters"} :step/id #uuid "15db0382-7d51-5840-b3da-75384a89354d" :step/math-academy-id 476522 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205039}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205039 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204090 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203853 :question/id #uuid "cba9196c-fdc3-594d-a1ad-948cf75bd366" :question/math-academy-id "e-17213"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0e7426e-553d-5f20-811f-15f3a5ea0056" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204596} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297177} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508071}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter"} :step/id #uuid "8211e2