ef 17592186477066}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter - Variables and Constants on Both Sides"} :step/id #uuid "eddfa8ae-b5d6-5ef0-ba45-f6c57207ae16" :step/math-academy-id 476529}] :activity/title "Linear Equations With No Solutions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186204177}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565489} :activity/id #uuid "c337d6eb-3aa0-55bd-86e4-7976b5c91d04" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050884 :topic/id #uuid "6e36fc4f-7ad7-519b-88fa-4bc240d36c28" :topic/math-academy-id 5572} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565489 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204598 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0f08b77-eeef-5fc2-8543-09a2276b46e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203498} {:answer/id #uuid "ed88b415-ec31-5753-9538-94d29a0edac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203499} {:answer/id #uuid "06e406e9-458f-5b5b-8a76-47ae321cb09d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203500} {:answer/id #uuid "217b4c73-d235-5933-8a9f-ef748ad498ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203501} {:answer/id #uuid "7cc66499-02a0-5560-9357-f491dc4ae661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203502}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203502} :answer-field/id #uuid "204618ec-8108-58ed-8408-6f51fe3d50c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c5eb239a-6da7-5d84-946d-c53824d57aa5" :question/math-academy-id "q-287677" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n2px=3x+5\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $ax=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $3x$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2px & =3x+5 \\\\ 2px-3x & =3x+5-3x \\\\ 2px-3x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $x$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(2p-3)x=5.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ax=b:$\n\n$$\n{{(2p-3)}_{⏟}}_{a}x={{5}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2p-3=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2p-3 & =0 \\\\ 2p-3+3 & =0+3 \\\\ 2p & =3 \\\\ \\frac{2p}{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1866355e-5a27-511e-8e09-7bbdcb7e2ecf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565540}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565519 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297202 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "488785de-3552-5c44-b37c-f80db84151a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295682}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295682} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f518425-6bb3-513e-9a2f-cd6d4cd2d157" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e9e21b0c-1002-52c2-8ff0-0958a8c28640" :question/math-academy-id "q-288644" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $v,$ given by\n\n$$\n(k-2)v-3=5\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $av=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k-2)v-3 & =5 \\\\ (k-2)v-3+3 & =5+3 \\\\ {{(k-2)}_{⏟}}_{a}v & ={{8}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nk-2=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k-2 & =0 \\\\ k & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9195a63d-152b-58e5-95a9-d8c8dcb4ee50"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565540 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297197 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "298bad1f-9367-5a05-95d2-933a09daee2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295501} {:answer/id #uuid "3dad73b7-975d-5246-83b7-9657d612712e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295623} {:answer/id #uuid "a52921f6-35e3-5fd6-831b-5c76a68fe597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296138} {:answer/id #uuid "c10c0188-b833-5221-be57-af73a4dae17b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296286} {:answer/id #uuid "f34978c9-f088-5aab-b438-960ba47ee1ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296559}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296138} :answer-field/id #uuid "aab0add4-30b0-59ee-8400-8a7cc1beaf29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1328ee8c-fdcc-59c5-a877-6291af207d84" :question/math-academy-id "q-288596" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $y,$ given by\n\n$$\n(q+9)y=-6\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $q?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ay=b.$ In this case,\n\n$$\na=q+9,\\,b=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nq+9=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q+9 & =0 \\\\ q & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f4a9ce2d-f2d1-5c3c-aac6-760f7e33d1b4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565519}}] :activity/title "Linear Equations With No Solutions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564936}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069776} :activity/id #uuid "3fcb435e-dc90-5888-b34e-40125c4a37f1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050887 :topic/id #uuid "6e563fb5-2e53-5b68-a90b-a963f8d0dc96" :topic/math-academy-id 390} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069776 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069998 :tutorial/content "To simplify radical expressions, we can use the quotient rule for radicals, which states \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\sqrt {a}} {\\sqrt{b}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac a b}.\n$$\n\nFor example, let's simplify $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{16w^2}{25}}$\n\nFirst, we put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{16w^2}{25}}  = \\dfrac {\\sqrt{16w^2}} {\\color{blue}\\sqrt{25}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{16w^2}}{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{5^2}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{16w^2}}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression under the radical as a perfect square and taking its square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{16{w}^{2}}}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{(4w)^{2}}}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{|4w|}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{|4|\\cdot |w|}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{4\\cdot |w|}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{4|w|}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7bfc07d9-feb0-5bcc-b3f0-50bb42eb2b6d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6691 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2bc45b7b-b768-5e67-92eb-6d8dfdb26080" :step/math-academy-id 40513 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069777}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069777 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068836 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068442 :question/id #uuid "dd7c79e5-d274-5f04-af44-335b5a6d95df" :question/math-academy-id "e-10699"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc723e84-3051-5e73-ac57-08da7193c368" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050496}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503672}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Radical Expressions With Perfect Squares Under the Radical"} :step/id #uuid "92bbfce1-1705-5e28-a0bf-e544d5fecdf9" :step/math-academy-id 40520 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069780}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069778 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069999 :tutorial/content "The quotient rule for radicals also works for higher-order roots, like the cube root:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{ \\dfrac a b } = \\dfrac{ \\sqrt[3]{a} }{ \\sqrt[3]{b} }\n$$\n\nIn general, we have the following result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{ \\dfrac a b } = \\dfrac{ \\sqrt[n]{a} }{ \\sqrt[n]{b} }\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a1d735da-05d5-5959-9226-229b7c29c497" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6692 :tutorial/title "The Quotient Rule for Cube Roots and Other Radicals"} :step/id #uuid "57fbfe53-6167-5a8c-ac3b-d190aa02b687" :step/math-academy-id 40523} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069779 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068837 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068443 :question/id #uuid "40af3166-710b-508c-9a8e-2c54365cd238" :question/math-academy-id "e-10712"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f03bcc0e-23e1-534b-81bb-146079d37c3e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542226}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Radical Expression With Domain Restrictions"} :step/id #uuid "25606295-3ea3-509a-91f7-294ef43bb1c1" :step/math-academy-id 40568 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069778}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069780 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068838 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068444 :question/id #uuid "9ceac090-9a1c-57d7-a311-f771ece6e0b6" :question/math-academy-id "e-10716"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eeca0971-59ed-576d-a248-293decd655b1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048132}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542186}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Radical Expressions With Perfect Fourths Under the Radical"} :step/id #uuid "d917615e-b6ee-5929-8832-7bcfe670df91" :step/math-academy-id 40586 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069779}}] :activity/title "Simplifying Square Root Expressions Using the Quotient Rule" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068937}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542270} :activity/id #uuid "ee3bbf0a-d993-58e5-a8fc-07c4a231168f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050887 :topic/id #uuid "6e563fb5-2e53-5b68-a90b-a963f8d0dc96" :topic/math-academy-id 390} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542239 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542186 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2eb4d114-2918-5ab2-bf22-75e0da8cb4b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12|b|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541515} {:answer/id #uuid "909bae66-0894-556e-9500-12a1b7a472c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{b}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541718} {:answer/id #uuid "b637ac06-ccda-5d32-8eda-5fa75421a3e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3{b}^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541796} {:answer/id #uuid "cb31de31-8d0b-5162-a0c9-422a314cd031" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3{b}^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541853} {:answer/id #uuid "de41d849-2235-524b-857c-75638f3eee68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3|b|}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541893}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541796} :answer-field/id #uuid "db7da12b-df2d-5c36-a689-024827fa16bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ad438bd0-4fe5-5891-83d5-f58699093a97" :question/math-academy-id "q-162925" :question/problem "$\\sqrt{\\frac{9{b}^{4}}{16}}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\frac{9{b}^{4}}{16}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{9{b}^{4}}}{\\sqrt{16}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{9{b}^{4}}}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{9{b}^{4}}}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{9{b}^{4}}}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{(3{b}^{2})^{2}}}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{|3{b}^{2}|}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{|3|\\cdot |{b}^{2}|}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\cdot |{b}^{2}|}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\cdot |{b}^{2}|}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\cdot {b}^{2}}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{3{b}^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1bd8041d-54cf-5609-a2c4-badf0fb9dad3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542246}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542242 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542127 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c7b78ca-8580-5c9a-9e23-bd4f302f7745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4{q}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541551} {:answer/id #uuid "52b37762-6860-50dd-aeb0-d0cabcd4e494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8q}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541590} {:answer/id #uuid "5b54469f-3889-528d-887c-1afa870e8a1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4q}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541603} {:answer/id #uuid "685b98ba-a8e1-5c4f-b9d8-047835caaa45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{8q}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541632} {:answer/id #uuid "9043e801-59d7-5790-8af9-6f696592f319" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{8{q}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541715}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541590} :answer-field/id #uuid "e404fc04-8fb2-5959-b21b-6f275023d337" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "21c2ef3e-e8f5-5baf-b96b-0dcf270605ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-162953" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{64{q}^{2}}}$ given that $q>0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{64{q}^{2}}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{9}}{\\sqrt{64{q}^{2}}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}}}{\\sqrt{64{q}^{2}}}=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{64{q}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{64{q}^{2}}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(8q)^{2}}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{|8q|} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{|8|\\cdot |q|} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{8\\cdot |q|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we are told that $q>0,$ so we have $|q|=q.$ Therefore, the final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8\\cdot |q|} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{8\\cdot q} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{8q}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "26cd2bb1-3bcf-502c-a435-ef5e74ba0530"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542246 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542155 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cdfb1d6-c6c1-5db2-a980-ce6c3297090a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541608} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f4cc0a-9998-5ff9-9843-1a1abb3513fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541837} {:answer/id #uuid "cf54f03f-7bc1-5fbd-ba32-a303174a4ad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541861} {:answer/id #uuid "d71cb3dd-3e7f-5450-8b7f-a549af417b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541882} {:answer/id #uuid "e7c6bcbf-624c-5a0d-bf2e-2dc05e823676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541914}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541914} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6085066-0ecc-5f31-ba69-929db19f889c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6a46c4fe-4fbf-584a-9b57-cb0bbf472594" :question/math-academy-id "q-162933" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\sqrt{125{t}^{4}}}{\\sqrt{5}}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring the quotient under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{125{t}^{4}}}{\\sqrt{5}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{125{t}^{4}}{5}}=\\sqrt{25{t}^{4}}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{25{t}^{4}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(5{t}^{2})^{2}} & = \\\\ |5{t}^{2}| & = \\\\ |5|\\cdot |{t}^{2}| & = \\\\ 5\\cdot |{t}^{2}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot |{t}^{2}| & = \\\\ 5\\cdot {t}^{2} & = \\\\ 5{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "311b2fda-a010-566e-93e7-acf12c4fd8a2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542242}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542270 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542226 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d11c2d2-3001-5e8a-be3a-e32230076db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541553} {:answer/id #uuid "98d13ba9-dfd3-52c2-b70f-fb7735e5b240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541740} {:answer/id #uuid "a852ddcc-f65d-5be4-bb4e-4120b2627298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541768} {:answer/id #uuid "bf0b0421-5aec-5d9c-a383-f76749bb0f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541824} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c3f8b1-179e-5104-b995-51741f073176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541941}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541941} :answer-field/id #uuid "18f8115a-b5d4-5929-a1c8-36a6a9737a96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f043c885-cf65-5aa1-b5b8-12a5b94bca34" :question/math-academy-id "q-152384" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{\\sqrt{32{z}^{5}}}{\\sqrt{8{z}^{3}}}$ given that $z>0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring the quotient under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{32{z}^{5}}}{\\sqrt{8{z}^{3}}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{32{z}^{5}}{8{z}^{3}}}=\\sqrt{4{z}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4{z}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{2}^{2}{z}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{(2z)}^{2}} & = \\\\ |2z| & = \\\\ |2|\\cdot |z| & = \\\\ 2\\cdot |z| & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we are told that $z>0,$ so we have $|z|=z.$ Therefore, the final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot |z| & = \\\\ 2\\cdot z & = \\\\ 2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "87aa8fbe-097a-54cb-9c88-e60e947273cc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542239}}] :activity/title "Simplifying Square Root Expressions Using the Quotient Rule" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541423}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060674} :activity/id #uuid "780b6d06-4e3c-5fb9-a687-1ad2687e0771" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050953 :topic/id #uuid "7525f3f4-fbe5-5024-8fab-55ad3066edd8" :topic/math-academy-id 266} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060580 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059706 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059519 :question/id #uuid "935e8644-8d66-5be6-87ec-b8176675aa91" :question/math-academy-id "e-4233"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4af25d77-e2aa-585a-971f-693c44f34e48" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050749}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503715}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Variable in One Denominator"} :step/id #uuid "a0e30d07-9c41-5c2c-839c-9e49ca64764c" :step/math-academy-id 15458 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060581}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060581 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059707 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059520 :question/id #uuid "e7d11d09-773c-58a9-8582-2cd47a3b141c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4234"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93665eb6-5d3e-52d2-afc6-bc27488190e1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540075}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial in One Denominator"} :step/id #uuid "233132be-0fc4-52d1-9152-818b8c45b168" :step/math-academy-id 15459 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060582}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060582 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059708 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059521 :question/id #uuid "3ae175a8-7654-57c8-b5d8-33d7892b7a24" :question/math-academy-id "e-4235"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c24d70-3bde-5277-bc92-ae081065deee" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048725}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503729}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial and a Monomial in Denominators"} :step/id #uuid "13e292aa-c881-5526-9904-6d174bbb856b" :step/math-academy-id 15460 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060583}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060583 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059709 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059522 :question/id #uuid "13f9fd01-802e-5331-b422-b9aa2cdda7ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-4236"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "387a794f-17ea-5ea9-86a1-656a495f62cb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048213}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540100}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with Binomials in Both Denominators"} :step/id #uuid "634f203a-900b-5f9f-977b-e1bb2ab2c5ab" :step/math-academy-id 15461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060674 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060937 :tutorial/content "Remember that to solve a rational equation, such as the one below, we can use cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x} & =\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 5\\cdot 2 & =3\\cdot x \\\\ 10 & =3x \\\\ \\frac{10}{3} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, there is also another method that we can use, known as the **flip method,** which involves taking the reciprocal of both sides of an equation. So, when an equation involves two fractions, we can \"flip\" them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nThe flip method is useful because it allows us to remove a variable from a denominator. For example, applying the flip method, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}x}=\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}5}  \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\color{blue}x}{\\color{red}2} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the resulting equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot \\frac{x}{2} & =2\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\cancel{2}} & =2\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{10}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "33bd96be-8ef8-525c-be19-8590b4893b52" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3123 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b043ea3a-fd3a-5de4-b7ac-21a29627ca53" :step/math-academy-id 17016 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060580}}] :activity/title "Solving Rational Equations Using the Flip Method" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059941}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540116} :activity/id #uuid "1c136286-07e7-54b0-87c2-d0c00e24e994" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050953 :topic/id #uuid "7525f3f4-fbe5-5024-8fab-55ad3066edd8" :topic/math-academy-id 266} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540116 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9afe93af-c815-5f2c-ac75-9a584d561ee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058879} {:answer/id #uuid "e4d50060-ed5a-55ba-98a5-e136d622f6bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058880} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d7edad-f24a-5a39-b3a9-bdf0e222d37a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058881} {:answer/id #uuid "3af8e372-8b94-5bca-966e-b13590d4c251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058882} {:answer/id #uuid "4335e387-f8cb-50d1-a3a5-f1a739c9876b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058883}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186058882} :answer-field/id #uuid "084ec80c-c0ed-58a4-b621-614f03d00659" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "09320d7a-d867-531b-9740-66504912149d" :question/math-academy-id "q-67332" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{3x+2}=-\\frac{4}{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+2}{2}=-\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{6x+4}{4}=-\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot \\frac{6x+4}{4} & =4\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{4}) \\\\ 6x+4 & =-x \\\\ 6x+x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "04bd31c0-ddc2-5b9c-ad07-d73251e35927" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540140}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540123 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d56b3b6-d2ee-5a6a-ac79-f166c602aad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539536} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbc6a18-26ef-5ee7-a216-046a93109cdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539634} {:answer/id #uuid "684d8919-d630-53ec-be88-da83985b9cfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539650} {:answer/id #uuid "adf72623-7732-5460-b0b6-5cc1011adb27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539775} {:answer/id #uuid "f71975b1-e674-5e94-a227-f47c16e02797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539894}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539894} :answer-field/id #uuid "746714c2-cc0f-5a43-9606-b934d8793273" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dba16e2f-f755-5c6f-b25b-801d348b9282" :question/math-academy-id "q-67435" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-\\frac{4}{x-2}=\\frac{1}{x-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=2$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{x-1}{1}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator, $4.$ The equation becomes\n\n$$\n-\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{4x-4}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)\\cdot (\\frac{x-2}{-4}) & =(-4)\\cdot \\frac{4x-4}{4} \\\\ x-2 & =-4x+4 \\\\ x+4x & =4+2 \\\\ 5x & =6 \\\\ (\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot (5x) & =(\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot (6) \\\\ (\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})\\cdot (\\cancel{5}x) & =(\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot (6) \\\\ x & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "128aaed4-1578-549e-bc3a-0ca44706efdb"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540140 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540075 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30e054d1-ef35-5151-b5a6-95077b0323c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539572} {:answer/id #uuid "5deed0da-0520-5246-8e6c-365d802468b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539637} {:answer/id #uuid "6c5af59e-eefa-5320-9d2c-7a4fb55193a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539660} {:answer/id #uuid "a816dc9c-912f-5b96-8ae4-1b55000c74ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539765} {:answer/id #uuid "b4e754c7-eafd-5c39-98a7-4f2732e90d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539791}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539660} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7cb7b4d-8e3a-52e0-b39c-57977a95c75f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9929bc33-2631-5ab2-871b-80ee305fc700" :question/math-academy-id "q-67413" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{x+5}=\\frac{1}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=-5$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+5}{1}=\\frac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\nx+5=3\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =3 \\\\ x+5-5 & =3-5 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5a1efe66-123d-5aba-9cc1-514789a8cab9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540123}}] :activity/title "Solving Rational Equations Using the Flip Method" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539465}]}
